  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 150 PM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nThunderstorms with heavy rainfall continue across southwestern \nMinnesota early this afternoon as a shortwave comes across the top \nof the central CONUS ridge. Areas along and south of the Minnesota \nRiver from roughly Granite Falls to Mankato have received 1-2\" of \nrain thanks to nearly stationary storms that continue to bubble up \nwithout really going anywhere. The bulk of the heaviest rain has \nremained just outside of the CWA into far southwestern Minnesota and \nnorthern Iowa. A Flood Watch has been issued for our two \nsouthwestern-most counties in collaboration with the Sioux Falls and \nDes Moines offices. Elsewhere over the past hour or so, there's been \nan uptick in shower and thunderstorms further north towards I-94.\nThese also seem pretty pulse-y much like the ones to the south \nand are expected to diminish by this evening. Storms are not \nexpected to make it much further east than they are currently with\nample dry air in the low/mid levels. This is apparent when \nlooking at the difference in PWATS from eastern Minnesota \n(1.3\"ish) to western Minnesota (1.7 to 1.9\"ish). CAMs have \nstruggled with the evolution of the ongoing storms most of the \nday due to the chaotic storm motion (or lack of), but the \ngeneral consensus is that precip remains to the west of a line \nfrom roughly St. Cloud to Mankato.\n\nFollowing today's showers and storms, a deepening low across the \nDakotas will move into southern Manitoba and Saskatchewan late \nMonday. In response, southeast winds are expected to become\nbreezy tomorrow with gusts around 30 MPH ahead of a cold front.\nThis front is expected to sweep across Minnesota Monday evening\ninto Tuesday, bringing our next chance for showers and storms. \nNorthern Minnesota will likely see the bulk of the (much needed)\nQPF, but central Minnesota down through the Twin Cities into \nsoutheastern Minnesota and western Wisconsin may still end up \nwith around an inch of rain by the time the system moves out \nTuesday. Wednesday looks to be the coolest day of the period \nwith highs in the low to mid 70s under strong CAA. This will \nlikely make for another breezy day with northwest winds gusting \nto around 30 MPH. The remainder of the work week looks mainly \ndry with highs in the upper 70s to low 80s. Looking ahead to \nnext weekend, a pattern shift is likely on the way with the \narrival of a series of troughs. This will lead to an uptick in \nrain chances and more seasonable temperatures for mid September.\nBeyond next weekend, there seems to be agreement among \nensembles that highs in the 60s and 70s with lows in the 40s and\n50s will become more and more common as the fall equinox \napproaches.",
